  (3) THE PHANTOM'S OWN SHAPE.  rho_ph = div[(nu-1) g_N]/(4 pi G).  Its isodensity axis ratio is what a
      galaxy-galaxy lensing halo-ellipticity measurement reports.  Computed on an (R,z) grid:
    bright spiral y(4Rd) ~ 1    : q(r=4R_d) = 0.239  q(r=8R_d) = 0.720  q(r=16R_d) = 0.983
    dwarf  y(4Rd) ~ 0.1         : q(r=4R_d) = 0.233  q(r=8R_d) = 0.706  q(r=16R_d) = 0.985
    (q < 1 means the phantom is OBLATE -- flattened toward the disc, as the halving law requires.)
    CAVEAT, recorded against interest: for a RAZOR-THIN disc part of the phantom is a delta-function sheet of
    surface density (nu-1) Sigma_b, so the innermost axis ratios above are dominated by that sheet and are an
    artefact of the idealisation, not a halo shape.  The honest, sheet-free statement is the FIELD contrast in
    part (2): the phantom's angular structure is 1+L times the baryons' and both DIE OFF outward, because a
    point mass's phantom is spherical.  At r = 16 R_d the modified field's pole-to-equator contrast is only
    2.8% (Newtonian 5.2%), i.e. a halo ellipticity of ~1.4% -- one to two orders of magnitude below what
    galaxy-galaxy lensing halo-ellipticity measurements can currently resolve.  That is this candidate's
    limiting number and it is what caps its promise.
    LambdaCDM alternative: N-body haloes are triaxial with q = 0.7-0.9 but their orientation is set by the
    tidal field, not by the disc, and lensing measurements report f_h = e_halo/e_light with no predicted value.

  (4) THE UPSILON LEVER.  The ratio contrast(obs)/contrast(bar) = 1 + L(y) is a RATIO of two anisotropies, so
      every amplitude -- Upsilon, distance, the disc's mass -- cancels from it exactly.  Upsilon survives only
      inside y:
      Upsilon = 0.3  ->  y scales to 0.180  ->  1 + L = 0.5986
      Upsilon = 0.5  ->  y scales to 0.300  ->  1 + L = 0.6245
      Upsilon = 0.7  ->  y scales to 0.420  ->  1 + L = 0.6446
      d log(1+L) / d log Upsilon = +0.0875   (compare: deep-tail a_0 rung -0.647, KiDS dwarf stack -1.046)
  [PASS] Upsilon lever on the halving law |d log(1+L)/d log Upsilon| < 0.15   (+0.0875)

  (5) BOTH FOOTINGS.  1 + L depends on a_0 only through y = g_bar/a_0:
